# Deployment parameters for this rig — the single source of truth.
#
# Read by: server.py (via python-dotenv), mcp-run.sh (which launches the MCP server),
# the Makefile (via -include), and set_env.sh. Change a value here and every one of
# them follows. A real environment variable always wins over anything set here, so
# `GOOGLE_CLOUD_ZONE=us-central1-b make status` still works for a one-off.
#
# THIS RIG PROVISIONS THROUGH COMPUTE ENGINE, NOT THE CLOUD TPU API. It is the A/B twin of
# tpu-vllm-v6e1-2b: same chip, same checkpoint, same vLLM flags, different control plane.
# Keep the serving parameters below in step with that rig or the comparison stops being one.
#
# Zone note: europe-west4-a, changed from us-east5-b on 2026-08-10 after the first real
# provision. BOTH zones publish ct6e-standard-1t — that is not the discriminator. The
# discriminator is Compute Engine quota, which is regional and which this project holds in
# europe-west4 (CT6E=32) and NOT in us-east5 (no stated value). The 512 v6e chips this
# project holds in us-east5 are TPU API quota and are unreachable from this path.
#
# europe-west4-a granted a flex-start ct6e-standard-1t immediately, with no DWS queueing.
# It is also the zone the sibling rig's knee and overflow measurements were taken in.
# us-west4 has no v6e at all. See CLAUDE.md before changing.

GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=aisprint-491218
GOOGLE_CLOUD_REGION=europe-west4
GOOGLE_CLOUD_ZONE=europe-west4-a

MODEL_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it

# --- What Compute Engine actually consumes -----------------------------------------------
# MACHINE_TYPE is the real accelerator request on this path. ct6e-standard-1t is one v6e
# chip, 44 vCPU / 176 GB. There is a second family spelled <name>-tpu, identical in vCPU,
# memory and zone coverage but reporting guestAcceleratorType tpu-v6e instead of ct6e; the
# two are not known to be interchangeable, so this is config rather than a derived string.
MACHINE_TYPE=ct6e-standard-1t
# Replaces the TPU API's --runtime-version. Pin the FAMILY, never a dated build: images ship
# roughly weekly and every superseded build goes DEPRECATED within the fortnight.
IMAGE_FAMILY=ubuntu-accel-2204-amd64-tpu-v5e-v5p-v6e
IMAGE_PROJECT=ubuntu-os-accelerator-images
# The image default is 10GB, which cannot hold the vLLM TPU image. Undersizing fails late,
# after boot, during the docker pull.
BOOT_DISK_SIZE_GB=200

# ACCELERATOR_TYPE is documentation here — the Cloud TPU API's spelling, kept so benchmark
# reports and the directory name line up with the sibling rig. NEVER passed to gcloud on
# this path; `gcloud compute instances create` would reject it.
ACCELERATOR_TYPE=v6e-1
TENSOR_PARALLEL_SIZE=1

# Serving parameters. See SERVING-PARAMS.md for the decision and its evidence.
# Held identical to tpu-vllm-v6e1-2b on purpose — this rig varies the control plane and
# nothing else, so any difference here would confound the comparison.
MAX_MODEL_LEN=32768

# --- Provisioning -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# flex-start, spot, on-demand, or reservation-bound. These are this rig's lowercase labels;
# server.py maps them to gcloud's SCREAMING_CASE --provisioning-model values. Every creation
# tool takes a provisioning_model argument that overrides this per call.
#   flex-start        - FLEX_START. Queues for scarce capacity via Dynamic Workload
#                       Scheduler, then self-deletes at MAX_RUN_DURATION.
#   spot              - SPOT. Reclaimable with ~30s notice. In us-east5 v6e spot lists
#                       DEARER than flex-start; read estimate_deployment_cost, don't assume.
#   on-demand         - STANDARD. Full price, no preemption.
#   reservation-bound - RESERVATION_BOUND. Consumes a calendar or dense-deployment
#                       reservation. HAS NO QUEUED RESOURCE EQUIVALENT — only on this path.
PROVISIONING_MODEL=flex-start
# How long to keep asking for flex-start capacity, and how long the VM may run once granted.
# Unlike the TPU API, --max-run-duration is not flex-start-only here: spot and on-demand get
# an automatic stop too, paired with --instance-termination-action=DELETE.
REQUEST_VALID_FOR=2h
MAX_RUN_DURATION=4h
# The reservation RESERVATION_BOUND consumes. Required for that model and ignored by the other
# three: gcloud accepts RESERVATION_BOUND only alongside --reservation-affinity=specific, so
# the model on its own is not a complete request. Empty because this project holds no future
# reservation yet. Create one out of band — it is a separate resource, not a create-time flag:
#   gcloud compute future-reservations create <name> --reservation-mode=CALENDAR \
#     --tpu-version=V6E --chip-count=1 --workload-type=SERVING --zone=europe-west4-a \
#     --start-time=<RFC3339> --duration=1d --planning-status=SUBMITTED \
#     --require-specific-reservation --reservation-name=<name>
# Calendar mode is 1-90 days, visible 120 days ahead, and CANNOT be modified or cancelled once
# submitted. v6e calendar zones: asia-northeast1-b, europe-west4-a, us-east5-a/-b, us-south1-ai1b.
# A reservation is pinned to its zone, so GOOGLE_CLOUD_ZONE must match it.
RESERVATION_NAME=

# --- Quota: the trap this rig exists to document -------------------------------------------
# The two control planes meter against DIFFERENT POOLS. Verified 2026-08-10: this project
# holds 512 v6e chips in us-east5 under the TPU API and NO STATED VALUE for family CT6E
# there under the Compute Engine quota this path consumes. TPU API quota does not carry over.
#
# It does NOT follow that the project has no Compute Engine v6e quota — it holds CT6E=32 in
# eight regions (europe-west4, asia-east1, asia-northeast1, asia-south1, asia-southeast1,
# southamerica-east1, southamerica-west1, us-south1) and none in us-east1/us-east5/us-west1.
# The trap is that the pools are disjoint and regionally misaligned, not that one is empty.
#
# WHICH ID GOVERNS WHICH MODEL — corrected 2026-08-11, an earlier version had this wrong.
# Google's Compute Engine provisioning-models page states it outright:
#   "When you create a Flex-start VM, preemptible quota is consumed."
#   https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/provisioning-models
# So FLEX_START spends GCE_SPOT_QUOTA_ID first even though flex-start is not preemptible in
# behaviour (once granted it runs uninterrupted). This rig defaults to flex-start, so the
# preemptible metric is the one that gates it in practice — but see the fallback below.
#
#   FLEX_START  -> GCE_SPOT_QUOTA_ID first, GCE_QUOTA_ID as documented FALLBACK
#   SPOT        -> GCE_SPOT_QUOTA_ID         (preemptible pool)
#   STANDARD    -> GCE_QUOTA_ID              (regional, family-wide, tpu_family=CT6E)
#
# The full sentence from that page, which matters because the second half is easy to miss:
#   "When you create a Flex-start VM, preemptible quota is consumed. If your project lacks
#    preemptible quota, then standard quota is consumed."
# So a flex-start region is usable if EITHER pool has room. Do not write off a region on one
# listing alone.
#
# There is no non-preemptible per-generation v6e id at all, which is why on-demand falls
# back to the generic family quota. v4/v5e/v5p each publish their own pair; v6e does not.
#
# The preemptible id is used at REGION scope: the per-zone spelling
# (PREEMPTIBLE-TPU-V6E-per-project-zone) exists but carries no entries in this project,
# while the per-region one holds the real values — 1536 in most regions, 32 in us-east5,
# 0 in us-east4 (read 2026-08-11). Defaults differ between the two ids and this trips
# people up: a region absent from the family quota inherits 0, a region absent from the
# preemptible quota inherits 1536.
#
# Requests denied in us-central1 (family), us-west1 (family) and us-east4 (both) were retried
# at 32 and again at 8 chips on 2026-08-11 — denied every time, within seconds, no partial
# grant. The ask size is not the variable; the region is. Do not retry them.
#
# DO NOT RE-REQUEST us-east5 PREEMPTIBLE. It sits at 32 where other regions have 1536, and
# that is not a mistake to correct — an increase to 1536 was submitted 2026-08-11 and came
# back "Quota request denied" within seconds. 32 is what this project is given there. The
# same 0 -> 32 ask was APPROVED in us-east5 and us-east1 and DENIED in us-central1,
# us-west1 and us-east4, so grants are a property of the region, not of the number asked
# for. 32 chips is 32x what this single-chip rig needs; leave it alone.
GCE_QUOTA_ID=TPUS-PER-TPU-FAMILY-per-project-region
GCE_SPOT_QUOTA_ID=PREEMPTIBLE-TPU-V6E-per-project-region
GCE_TPU_FAMILY=CT6E
# The sibling rig's ids, kept only so comparison tooling can report both. Compute Engine
# does not consult them.
TPU_QUOTA_ID=TPUV6EPerProjectPerZoneForTPUAPI
TPU_SPOT_QUOTA_ID=TPUV6EPreemptiblePerProjectPerZoneForTPUAPI

# Name of the instance this rig provisions. On this path the instance IS the node — there is
# no Queued Resource indirection and no derived <id>-node. Left unset it derives from the rig
# directory (gce-vllm-v6e1-2b). Set it only to reach something created under an older name;
# a rename otherwise orphans it and it keeps billing.
#INSTANCE_NAME=gce-vllm-v6e1-2b
#SERVICE_NAME=gce-vllm-v6e1-2b

# Name this rig's MCP server advertises, and the key the client must register it under —
# that key prefixes every tool, as mcp__<name>__find_tpu. Left unset it derives from the rig
# directory (gce-vllm-v6e1-2b), which is what keeps it distinguishable from the TPU-API twin
# registered as tpu-vllm-v6e1-2b. `make mcp-config` writes a .mcp.json using the same value.
#MCP_SERVER_NAME=gce-vllm-v6e1-2b
